#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068546 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068910 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76450310-bc86-5f3b-9bb8-82206d89c627" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Left and Right Limits at an Interior Point"}] :question/id #uuid "dbad1aa8-5786-524e-9add-5295231dfd83" :question/math-academy-id "e-3557" :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0^-} f(x)$ and $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 0^+} f(x)$ for the function given below.\n\n![](images/1d/1dc9fd5dc473e15939a50fbb209475d580be540fb8c9a2bc8602a6a9724b08bd.png)" :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $0$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^{-}}f(x) = 1 \\, .\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $0$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^{+}}f(x) = 1 \\, .\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^{+}}f(x)=1 \\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068635 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50612b3c-6a02-5a91-86b3-bbd69c99604c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Equalities Involving Limits"}] :question/id #uuid "24c5fa9b-f91e-5088-9647-4923784c5917" :question/math-academy-id "e-7" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below? \n\nI. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^-}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)$\n\nII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)$\n\nIII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)$\n\n![](images/1c/1c94c7d99009c46023556ce0f4f662f5a90ad315048099394016390552ade809.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the limits in question.\n\nLooking at the graph, as $x$ approaches $0$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ However, as $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined. So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^-}f(x)=0, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\text{DNE} \\, .\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined, and as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)=\\text{DNE}, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)=0 \\, .\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\nI. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^-}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)$ becomes $0=0,$ which is true.\n\nII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)$ becomes $\\text{DNE}=0,$ which is false.\n\nIII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0^+}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)$ becomes $\\text{DNE}=\\text{DNE},$ which is false because we can't compare the undefined values.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068649 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068911 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1a14c94-c26b-5a3b-bbbb-6f122b5de9de" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Left and Right Limits at Endpoints"}] :question/id #uuid "b788cb59-f017-5ebe-9f67-f746517de260" :question/math-academy-id "e-772" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ (shown below) is defined on $x\\in [-2,2]$. Find the left-sided and right-sided limits for the function at $x=-2$ and $x=2$. \n\n![](images/96/963e4b2e7875c5f8156e3d11eee1851ec6b73fd296ccd4a269bbda139f4b895e.png)" :question/worked-solution "We see from the graph that, approaching $x=-2$ from the right, the function approaches the value $y=4$. However, we cannot approach $x=-2$ from the left, because the function is not defined left of $x=-2.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-2^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,,\\quad\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-2^{+}}f(x)=4 \\,.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand,  approaching $x=2$ from the left, the function approaches the value $y=2$. However, we cannot approach $x=2$ from the right, because the function is not defined right of $x=2.$ So we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2^{-}}f(x)=2\\,,\\quad\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,2^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE} \\,.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Here, $\\text{DNE}$ is simply short for \"does not exist\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068911 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1a14c94-c26b-5a3b-bbbb-6f122b5de9de" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Left and Right Limits at Endpoints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f63906b-d536-5800-9221-6a3236936f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=-1,$ ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529726} {:answer/id #uuid "6d6007a9-0a8d-51bb-aa96-f88a2935158e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},$ ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529857} {:answer/id #uuid "bb40da77-9493-57f8-81ff-407b3b027a6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},$ ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530049} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d40e96-d5e2-59e3-9710-e58888103289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530080} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8b2ee6-4859-523e-9aba-6da0a4144bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=-1,$ ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f63906b-d536-5800-9221-6a3236936f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=-1,$ ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529726} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c34f4e5-750e-5d27-a487-4438e118c498" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f85c0d08-ef45-5aca-a806-01e365550a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106429" :question/problem "![](images/52/52478c6cdcae0ba1e46b34eea8a4115f2f5f14d8973604c5ab589842bb7db262.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ (shown above) is defined on $x\\in [-4,1).$ Find the right-sided limit at $x=-4$ and the left-sided limit at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-4$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $-1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{4}^{+}}f(x)=-1\\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50612b3c-6a02-5a91-86b3-bbd69c99604c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Equalities Involving Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25e5f3ac-6d0f-5113-bfc8-7212b2f51ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529663} {:answer/id #uuid "3350670e-0708-5a65-8760-c36b78ace901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529694} {:answer/id #uuid "373cc9b6-5ba1-5f89-8767-f6478dee4f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529705} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac0a9ca-fc4d-5f48-9ca4-aabb3f225399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529920} {:answer/id #uuid "bd849b07-b5b4-557f-9537-a17eaa8ba010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "373cc9b6-5ba1-5f89-8767-f6478dee4f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529705} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bf8286a-48fb-5ac5-864d-0bb3e1b4c15c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ddfb9440-a491-530c-8883-b3b8364d490f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106434" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f01058e0460ac0de134f36ce8a83aada0a719565f78c640b713526ec1b1a3a99.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?  \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x)=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ approaches $-3$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=0,$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{3}^{+}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined, Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=-2$, and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)=-2\\,.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=0$, and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50612b3c-6a02-5a91-86b3-bbd69c99604c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Equalities Involving Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f6bdb5-79f3-5acf-9f2b-9230d9baf9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529591} {:answer/id #uuid "55f9cd12-5bf6-589a-87b8-08c39a5e9f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529788} {:answer/id #uuid "64f08c7a-c573-5faf-aad5-ddf460636e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529834} {:answer/id #uuid "67a628cb-3f24-511c-9ee5-fd6644c9f051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529839} {:answer/id #uuid "7b30b91a-fe7c-5fd1-8d01-b6cbe5bb70eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55f9cd12-5bf6-589a-87b8-08c39a5e9f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529788} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5692066-4d1c-50f0-b011-92ff8c2f668e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2e89b2a9-3a87-5f49-9315-671676fe1a51" :question/math-academy-id "q-106457" :question/problem "![](images/21/21cf8b9a6a94900b68a565a2d0915ba849379a134009c37db64bd5ff1eea2ac7.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the limits in question. \n\nAs $x$ approaches $-2$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=2\\,.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $-2$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=-2,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=-2\\,.\n$$\n\nWe cannot approach $x=1$ from the right because the function is not defined to the right of $x=1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we cannot approach $2$ from the left because the function is not defined to the left of $x=2.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1,$ and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- I. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)$ becomes $2=-2,$ which is false.\n\n- II. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ is false because neither one-sided limit exists.\n\n- III. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$ becomes $1=1,$ which is true.\n\nThus, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068910 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76450310-bc86-5f3b-9bb8-82206d89c627" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Left and Right Limits at an Interior Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fddcb65-0b3f-5376-bb41-38935b6ad19f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fddcb65-0b3f-5376-bb41-38935b6ad19f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529685} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f94b681-420a-5f84-b8ee-e7d6ec657549" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530228} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbe3cfc7-17ac-5090-b863-c19ac950e9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbe3cfc7-17ac-5090-b863-c19ac950e9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530124} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee1f9f9d-34b1-5294-af05-80a2cd312768" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2100ce73-4770-5a89-a8b5-3f4b9726facd" :question/math-academy-id "q-342097" :question/problem "![](images/78/781d755a292a18934af5b28436593262070527aa41da3bda092f979d388ef1f7.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=$  \n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=$  \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-2$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $-2$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068910 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76450310-bc86-5f3b-9bb8-82206d89c627" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Left and Right Limits at an Interior Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53d320bf-671c-5132-aad9-aed99b12b41d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53d320bf-671c-5132-aad9-aed99b12b41d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529779} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fffec79-9b7c-5465-b9d1-a4732de4ab53" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530240} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf35967a-ba19-53d4-bf98-6f45147add4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf35967a-ba19-53d4-bf98-6f45147add4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530101} :answer-field/id #uuid "83f8be5a-aa05-5525-aa41-66f7fec68c54" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "585630ea-edc2-5584-9703-3115d4a68d23" :question/math-academy-id "q-342118" :question/problem "![](images/44/441d472f60a9cc7415799032f66d2a618bf8419336f52aefb47f3b1ae7640875.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $3$ from the *left*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $3$ from the *right*, the function $f(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068911 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1a14c94-c26b-5a3b-bbbb-6f122b5de9de" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Left and Right Limits at Endpoints"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cc4a6d9-dfe0-5f37-99cb-23132a2d44d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cc4a6d9-dfe0-5f37-99cb-23132a2d44d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529638} :answer-field/id #uuid "d061e6e3-b59b-572b-aa97-b05d15013910" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530371} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7342b3f-0153-5cba-b7a5-6621f41aac43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7342b3f-0153-5cba-b7a5-6621f41aac43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530116} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6785073-50ec-528b-8583-c2326c8a4528" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6b36716-81d8-575e-98da-f48320938f53" :question/math-academy-id "q-342137" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd7ee98596021320d0f24d5b813de476ffabaa48156316091e5f23fed9e20d30.png)\n\nThe function $y=g(x)$ (shown above) is defined on $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ Find the left-sided limit at $x=-1$ and the right-sided limit at $x=2.$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50612b3c-6a02-5a91-86b3-bbd69c99604c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Equalities Involving Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f68545a-5789-5d26-b54a-b5c18869ee17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529727} {:answer/id #uuid "6a7c8fad-19aa-53fe-9102-1f62a0707917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529851} {:answer/id #uuid "c326c467-211d-5b87-823b-764014a39998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530068} {:answer/id #uuid "e266eb35-fcda-5866-88e8-df50dcf09a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530146} {:answer/id #uuid "e33bc3c9-1b04-56f0-b61b-ebf99ab2a3df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a7c8fad-19aa-53fe-9102-1f62a0707917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529851} :answer-field/id #uuid "08777f1a-cad8-551c-a333-cf1d993c4fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffd5c800-ccb9-56a9-9fa0-86118664e9d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-34846" :question/problem "![](images/11/11e1c9a550944fc8435fc0d6f17be7d172e0c6d8f82fc1af950896f6ec8176b6.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?  \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=1$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$  approaches $y=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. As $x$ approaches $2$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1$, Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function value $f(x)$ is undefined. Consequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nInstead, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function value $f(x)$ approaches $y=1$, and consequently\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}]}
